
  The "simpler" argument for the hard cut is worth about three lines of code here, and it buys a worse result on exactly
  the strings this is for. [38;5;153mformat()[39m is a display utility — its output gets read by people — and a hard cut yields [38;5;153m"The [39m
  [38;5;153mquick brown fo..."[39m. The word-boundary version backs up to the last space and falls back to the hard cut when there's
  no space to back up to, so it's never [3mworse[0m, just occasionally the same.

  The real cost isn't complexity, it's that output length becomes variable: a budget of 20 might return 12 characters.
  If you're truncating to align fixed-width columns, that's disqualifying and you want the hard cut instead. Tell me if
  that's the use case and I'll flip the recommendation.

  [1mBehavior[0m ([38;5;153mtruncate: <number>[39m — the max length; numeric value, matching how [38;5;153mprefix[39m/[38;5;153msuffix[39m take their value directly):

  - [38;5;153mstr.length <= max[39m → returned unchanged, no ellipsis.
  - Otherwise: slice to [38;5;153mmax - 3[39m, back up to the last whitespace, trim trailing whitespace, append [38;5;153m'...'[39m. Result is ≤
    [38;5;153mmax[39m, per your answer.
  - No whitespace to back up to (first word exceeds the budget) → hard cut at [38;5;153mmax - 3[39m plus [38;5;153m'...'[39m.
  - [38;5;153mmax <= 3[39m → [38;5;153mstr.slice(0, max)[39m. There's no room for a meaningful ellipsis; the length guarantee is the thing worth
    keeping.

  [1mOne consequence of "ellipsis inside the budget"[0m, worth naming because it decides placement: truncation has to run
  [1mlast[0m, after [38;5;153mprefix[39m and [38;5;153msuffix[39m. Capping before them would let a suffix push the result back over [38;5;153mmax[39m and void the
  guarantee. The trade-off is that a long [38;5;153mprefix[39m eats the budget and can cut the suffix off entirely — correct given the
  guarantee you picked, but it means [38;5;153mtruncate[39m measures the [3mfinal[0m string, not the input.

  [1mNot doing:[0m an object-valued [38;5;153mtruncate: { maxLength, ellipsis, wordBoundary }[39m. Nothing here needs a configurable
  ellipsis yet, and a plain number matches the existing flat-option shape. Easy to widen later if a caller asks.

  [1mFiles touched:[0m [38;5;153mformat.js[39m (one block at the end of the pipeline), [38;5;153mformat.test.js[39m (new [38;5;153mconsole.assert[39m lines in the
  existing style — short string untouched, exact-length boundary, word-boundary cut, no-whitespace fallback, [38;5;153mmax <= 3[39m,
  and interaction with [38;5;153mprefix[39m). Verified with [38;5;153mnode format.test.js[39m; the repo has no test runner, so I'll stay with the
  [38;5;153mconsole.assert[39m pattern already there.

